WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:06.220 --> 00:00:11.700
This water is so clean, you can drink it.

00:00:12.160 --> 00:00:15.320
I've been drinking out of this river for
probably fifty years.

00:00:15.320 --> 00:00:16.580
What does it taste like?

00:00:17.160 --> 00:00:18.680
Tastes like water.

00:00:20.540 --> 00:00:23.419
That's because the
water here comes from one of the most

00:00:23.420 --> 00:00:26.040
protected places in the United States.

00:00:26.900 --> 00:00:28.840
You can't get here with a car.

00:00:28.840 --> 00:00:32.160
You can't use a boat with a motor.

00:00:32.160 --> 00:00:36.320
We couldn't even fly our drone past this point.

00:00:36.380 --> 00:00:41.560
These are the Boundary Waters of
northern Minnesota's Superior National Forest.

00:00:41.680 --> 00:00:44.340
Thousands of pristine lakes like this one.

00:00:45.100 --> 00:00:47.960
Hundreds of thousands of people come
to see it every year.

00:00:49.480 --> 00:00:50.740
You listen to the sounds of the rapids.

00:00:50.740 --> 00:00:52.660
You watch the eagle fly overhead.

00:00:54.080 --> 00:00:55.860
You paddle on still waters.

00:00:57.140 --> 00:00:58.060
Be on your own.

00:00:59.120 --> 00:01:01.400
But there's one problem.

00:01:01.520 --> 00:01:04.520
The Boundary Waters is just outside one of the largest

00:01:04.520 --> 00:01:06.820
untapped sources of copper in the world.

00:01:06.820 --> 00:01:09.520
Under the previous administration,

00:01:09.520 --> 00:01:13.120
America's rich natural resources,

00:01:13.120 --> 00:01:15.639
of which your state has a lot,

00:01:15.640 --> 00:01:17.580
were put under lock and key.

00:01:17.580 --> 00:01:20.640
Since taking office in 2017,

00:01:20.640 --> 00:01:24.259
the Trump administration
has opened up more than 13 million acres

00:01:24.259 --> 00:01:26.760
of public land for drilling and mining,

00:01:26.760 --> 00:01:30.440
that's more than any previous administration,

00:01:30.540 --> 00:01:33.259
including a part of Superior National Forest,

00:01:33.260 --> 00:01:36.420
right outside the Boundary Waters.

00:01:36.420 --> 00:01:39.380
Copper, the mineral underneath the forest, is the

00:01:39.380 --> 00:01:42.440
wiring in our phones, the pipes in our walls.

00:01:42.440 --> 00:01:45.100
And we also need it for electric car batteries,

00:01:45.100 --> 00:01:47.580
and solar panels, and wind turbines.

00:01:47.640 --> 00:01:49.120
We need copper,

00:01:49.120 --> 00:01:51.400
and there aren't that many places in the world to get it.

00:01:52.140 --> 00:01:56.540
All this has renewed a really old and complicated question:

00:01:57.020 --> 00:02:00.380
when is it worth
risking the life above ground

00:02:00.380 --> 00:02:03.640
for the riches underneath?

00:02:06.060 --> 00:02:10.260
The US has more than 600 million acres
of national parks,

00:02:10.260 --> 00:02:14.040
monuments, forests and wilderness areas.

00:02:14.620 --> 00:02:17.760
They are the brainchild of President Theodore Roosevelt.

00:02:18.720 --> 00:02:23.100
He worried that the reckless speed logging,
blast rock mining, and oil drilling that

00:02:23.100 --> 00:02:27.450
fueled the Industrial Revolution could
ruin the country's beauty and resources

00:02:27.450 --> 00:02:29.800
for future generations.

00:02:29.800 --> 00:02:34.290
So he created 150
national forests and parks, 18 national

00:02:34.290 --> 00:02:39.300
monuments, and 51 bird sanctuaries.

00:02:39.300 --> 00:02:41.400
I mean, get
up on a high mountain somewhere and

00:02:41.400 --> 00:02:45.720
remember that somebody saved that so
that you could have that experience and

00:02:45.720 --> 00:02:49.060
that's a kind of remarkable legacy.

00:02:49.660 --> 00:02:51.780
We call them drinking water lakes, because

00:02:51.780 --> 00:02:56.100
you can dip your cup right out the side
of your canoe and drink straight from

00:02:56.100 --> 00:02:58.910
the lakes without even treating them or
anything.

00:02:58.910 --> 00:03:02.970
Jason owns a business that outfits
visitors for canoe trips in the

00:03:02.970 --> 00:03:04.380
Boundary Waters.

00:03:04.380 --> 00:03:08.400
They come here because
what we have is so special and it's so

00:03:08.400 --> 00:03:13.230
unique you just can't you can't have
this sort of an experience anyplace else

00:03:13.230 --> 00:03:17.860
in the world. To have, you know, a million
acres totally undeveloped.

00:03:19.160 --> 00:03:22.340
The recreation and tourism industry here is big.

00:03:22.340 --> 00:03:25.280
it brings in about $77 million a year.

00:03:25.820 --> 00:03:29.980
The problem is, that's not
enough to support the entire region.

00:03:29.980 --> 00:03:36.000
Seasonal recreation workers typically
make about twenty-five thousand dollars a year.

00:03:36.000 --> 00:03:38.760
That's less than the state's
average income.

00:03:38.760 --> 00:03:41.540
You're not going to be able to raise a family on $25,000 a year.

00:03:41.540 --> 00:03:43.440
You're not even going to be able to buy a house.

00:03:43.780 --> 00:03:45.480
And this part of the state used to have

00:03:45.480 --> 00:03:48.800
a different core industry: iron mining.

00:03:48.800 --> 00:03:50.060
We've been mining up in this area for

00:03:50.060 --> 00:03:53.460
well over a hundred years, and so it has a big significance.

00:03:53.460 --> 00:03:57.980
There are lot of second, third, fourth, generation miners that have always worked in the

00:03:57.980 --> 00:04:00.220
mine or their family has worked in the
mine.

00:04:00.220 --> 00:04:01.640
The company that plans to build the

00:04:01.650 --> 00:04:05.849
mine near the Boundary Waters, Twin
Metals, has said they'll pay about $90,000

00:04:05.849 --> 00:04:10.680
a year, which is well over the state's average income.

00:04:10.680 --> 00:04:15.260
But copper mining is also risky in ways that
iron mining wasn't.

00:04:18.220 --> 00:04:21.135
For the last two years, the Twin Metals company has been

00:04:21.135 --> 00:04:22.710
collecting samples of the rock that they

00:04:22.710 --> 00:04:25.980
plan to mine near the Boundary Waters.

00:04:26.900 --> 00:04:28.979
This is a typical core sample, these

00:04:28.980 --> 00:04:34.000
little blocks that you're seeing in here
really establish how deep we are below

00:04:34.020 --> 00:04:35.640
ground surface.

00:04:35.640 --> 00:04:38.820
Once we hit this, 755 feet, this is

00:04:38.820 --> 00:04:42.560
where we start seeing the minerals.

00:04:43.240 --> 00:04:46.480
The copper is locked inside this shiny part here.

00:04:46.920 --> 00:04:51.500
To get it out, you have to crush up
the rock to a powder-like consistency

00:04:51.500 --> 00:04:58.820
copper only makes up about 1% of the
sample, which means 99% of it is waste.

00:04:59.340 --> 00:05:03.900
The crushed up rock is submerged in a
solution that floats the copper to the top.

00:05:03.900 --> 00:05:08.140
It's eventually what becomes wires,
pipes, and everything else.

00:05:08.660 --> 00:05:12.100
And the waste rock sinks. That's the risky part.

00:05:12.100 --> 00:05:16.020
It contains toxic elements like arsenic, lead, and mercury,

00:05:16.020 --> 00:05:18.580
which were previously
trapped inside the rock.

00:05:19.240 --> 00:05:21.860
And usually, when mining companies produce toxic waste,

00:05:21.860 --> 00:05:25.120
they store it in giant pits, like these.

00:05:25.120 --> 00:05:27.820
But those pits don't always hold up.

00:05:27.840 --> 00:05:30.800
It may be the worst environmental disaster

00:05:30.810 --> 00:05:32.958
in British Columbia's history.

00:05:32.960 --> 00:05:37.620
3 million gallon toxic stew of heavy metals poured downstream.

00:05:37.620 --> 00:05:40.360
Devastation as far as the eye can see.

00:05:40.370 --> 00:05:44.720
and the question that everyone here is
just stunned by is how this could ever

00:05:44.720 --> 00:05:46.620
have been allowed to happen.

00:05:46.620 --> 00:05:48.440
And even when there isn't one of these huge,

00:05:48.440 --> 00:05:51.300
catastrophic spills, abandoned mines

00:05:51.300 --> 00:05:54.060
leak millions of gallons of waste into streams.

00:05:54.060 --> 00:05:58.400
These colors indicate
heavy metal contamination that poisons

00:05:58.400 --> 00:06:01.040
aquatic life and taints drinking water.

00:06:01.500 --> 00:06:03.220
A lot of the economy that this region was

00:06:03.220 --> 00:06:06.480
based on was getting gold and silver out of
these hills

00:06:06.480 --> 00:06:08.720
and it left of a legacy of pollution.

00:06:09.480 --> 00:06:13.240
The cleanup costs taxpayers
millions of dollars long after mining

00:06:13.240 --> 00:06:16.540
companies take their profits and leave.

00:06:16.540 --> 00:06:18.300
Twin metals plans to store the waste

00:06:18.300 --> 00:06:24.000
from its mine right here: next to a river
that ultimately leads to the Boundary Waters.

00:06:24.000 --> 00:06:27.830
And instead of storing wastewater
in a pit, their plan is to dry out the

00:06:27.830 --> 00:06:30.960
waste and store it in stacks like these.

00:06:30.960 --> 00:06:33.139
On its website, Twin Metals calls the dry

00:06:33.140 --> 00:06:36.520
stack method "environmentally friendly,"

00:06:36.520 --> 00:06:40.660
but to support that, they point to another dry stack mine in Alaska,

00:06:40.680 --> 00:06:43.160
where the verdict isn't actually that clear.

00:06:43.160 --> 00:06:47.800
The Alaska mining company's own data
show that lead contaminated dust is

00:06:47.800 --> 00:06:53.540
blowing off the dry stacks, and they've
acknowledged that it could be getting into the water.

00:06:53.540 --> 00:06:56.479
And in aquatic life near
the mine, scientists found elevated

00:06:56.480 --> 00:07:00.080
levels of arsenic, lead, and mercury.

00:07:00.080 --> 00:07:01.789
Just like the Alaska mine, the Twin

00:07:01.789 --> 00:07:06.160
Metals mine would be surrounded by
interconnected waterways.

00:07:06.160 --> 00:07:11.220
Any pollution would spread far beyond the initial impact site.

00:07:11.220 --> 00:07:15.919
All this is why, in 2016, the Obama
administration decided the risks of

00:07:15.920 --> 00:07:22.060
copper mining here would be "unacceptable,"
and said that Twin Metals couldn't do it.

00:07:22.060 --> 00:07:27.100
But two years later, the Trump
administration reversed that decision.

00:07:27.100 --> 00:07:31.600
Tonight I'm proudly announcing that we
will soon be taking the first steps to

00:07:31.610 --> 00:07:37.810
rescind the federal withdrawal in
Superior National Forest and restore

00:07:37.810 --> 00:07:44.340
mineral exploration for our amazing
people and miners and workers.

00:07:46.040 --> 00:07:49.600
In the 1980s, the iron mines of northeastern Minnesota

00:07:49.600 --> 00:07:51.280
started to close.

00:07:51.280 --> 00:07:52.190
These days unemployment there

00:07:52.190 --> 00:07:54.320
has gone as high as 90%.

00:07:54.320 --> 00:07:57.080
Of the 15,000 union men and women who work the Iron

00:07:57.080 --> 00:08:02.320
Range mines, more than 3,000 are laid off
and hundreds more jobs are in jeopardy.

00:08:02.320 --> 00:08:07.180
A full-scale depression forcing thousands
of miners to abandon the area.

00:08:07.180 --> 00:08:10.060
When the layoffs happened in the mine,

00:08:10.060 --> 00:08:12.880
we were all hit. Everyone was hit, day care was hit,

00:08:12.880 --> 00:08:15.820
the hairdresser was hit, the
grocery store was hit,  not just the

00:08:15.830 --> 00:08:17.479
people that were laid off.

00:08:17.480 --> 00:08:21.380
That's because mining jobs tend to not stick around.

00:08:21.380 --> 00:08:25.380
I actually worked in several different
states in the mining industry.

00:08:25.380 --> 00:08:32.480
And one of the things I noticed, when I go back to
the places where I worked 20 years ago,

00:08:32.480 --> 00:08:35.100
none of those communities are thriving.

00:08:35.100 --> 00:08:39.220
You don't build long-term prosperity on a mining industry.

00:08:39.600 --> 00:08:42.580
Industry and
conservation have always fought over the

00:08:42.589 --> 00:08:47.300
best use of our public lands, and the
people closest to those lands often have

00:08:47.300 --> 00:08:49.960
differing visions for their own future.

00:08:49.960 --> 00:08:52.340
This proposed mine

00:08:52.340 --> 00:08:59.400
really puts the sustainable wilderness-edge economy, that we have going right now, at risk.

00:08:59.400 --> 00:09:05.720
And it definitely puts businesses like mine at risk.

00:09:05.740 --> 00:09:08.920
Jobs are scarce up here. Good jobs, I should say.

00:09:08.920 --> 00:09:12.800
Ones with benefits, where you
can raise a family, put money aside for your retirement.

00:09:12.800 --> 00:09:16.280
So this is a very good hope for us.

00:09:16.280 --> 00:09:19.000
For our towns, our families, our kids.

00:09:19.080 --> 00:09:22.900
In a speech in
1908, Teddy Roosevelt took stock of

00:09:22.910 --> 00:09:28.370
America's industrial progress. "We have
become great in a material sense because

00:09:28.370 --> 00:09:31.860
of the lavish use of our resources," he said.

00:09:31.860 --> 00:09:33.379
"But the time has come to inquire

00:09:33.379 --> 00:09:38.420
seriously what will happen when our
forests are gone, when the coal, the iron,

00:09:38.420 --> 00:09:41.620
the oil, and the gas are exhausted."

00:09:41.620 --> 00:09:43.660
More than a hundred years later,

00:09:43.660 --> 00:09:46.120
many of the most impressive human inventions,

00:09:46.120 --> 00:09:50.900
including those that could ultimately eliminate the need for fossil fuels,

00:09:50.900 --> 00:09:53.640
still depend on resources like copper.

00:09:53.640 --> 00:09:57.140
Resources that will run out someday.

00:09:57.140 --> 00:10:02.760
The question isn't really whether to let companies mine for copper near the Boundary Waters.

00:10:03.120 --> 00:10:11.300
It's whether the short-term gains are
worth changing places like this forever.

